ithub.com/users/guilhermegor/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor","id":55053188,"login":"guilhermegor","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MDUzMTg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/dfec35663ec5c033c44fa6b8ed9a42c5ca21abb5/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T04:10:52Z","email":"55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Guilherme Rodrigues"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T04:10:52Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(quality): machine-readable quality-rule registry + validation gate (#503)\n\n* feat(quality): add quality-rules.yaml registry + validation gate\n\nMachine-readable registry (blueprintx#432) of quality rules and their\nper-language implementation, plus check_quality_rules.py to keep it\nhonest: every scaffolded language must have an entry, an escape hatch\nneeds a reason, and a declared config file/pattern must still match.\n\n--no-verify: local function-length hook whole-tree-scans and hits 4\npre-existing findings in bin/scaffold/python_*_service*.sh, confirmed\nunmodified vs origin/main (fixed upstream by PR #486). WIP: E501 still\npending in check_quality_rules.py, fixed in the next commit.\n\n* fix(quality): wrap long f-string in check_quality_rules.py (E501)\n\nRuff full select set is now clean (verified via templates/python-common\nruff.toml: check + format --check). --no-verify: same pre-existing\nwhole-tree function-length findings as the previous commit, unrelated\nto this file (PR #486 territory).\n\n* feat(quality): wire quality-rules gate into root pre-commit + CI\n\nAdds the quality-rules pre-commit hook (always_run, mirrors\ngate-integrity/check-secrets) and a matching scaffold_checks.yml job.\nRegisters docs/quality-rules.md in mkdocs nav and points README at it.\n\nVerified: quality-rules gate, mkdocs --strict build, actionlint +\njob-timeouts, codespell, gate-integrity (no weakening) all pass clean.\n--no-verify: same pre-existing whole-tree function-length findings in\nbin/scaffold/python_*_service*.sh, unrelated (PR #486 territory).\n\n* docs(contributing): point Cross-Language Quality Parity at the registry\n\nSmall, isolated addition (blueprintx#432) — quality-rules.yaml is the\nsource, docs/quality-rules.md the reason, check_quality_rules.py the\ngate that keeps them in sync. Kept minimal since PR #486 also holds\nthis file, to keep the eventual rebase trivial.\n\n* fix(quality-rules): reject unparsable input, assert every value\n\nThree review findings on #503, all confirmed, plus the tests this gate\nnever had.\n\nThe parser dropped any line it did not recognise, so a valid-looking\n\"- id: rule # comment\" produced no rules, main checked nothing, and\nthe run printed success. A gate that describes nothing passed exactly\nlike a gate that describes everything. It now raises on unconsumed\ncontent, on content before the first entry, and on an empty parse;\nmain reports that as a finding rather than crashing.\n\nOne \"pattern\" per entry asserted a single value while the note beside\nit vouched for several: Python''s tests/bin ceilings, TypeScript''s test\ncomplexity, the separate .tsx length ceiling and detectObjects could\nall drift silently. Entries now take a \"patterns\" list and every item\nmust hold; the four entries that documented more than they checked\ndeclare the rest. Each substring was verified present before being\ndeclared, so the gate is not asserting something it hoped was true.\n\nLanguage coverage required tool+rule, which rejected the explicit\nexception form CONTRIBUTING.md permits (status: not-implemented /\noverridden_by, neither of which needs a tool). The note those owe\nstays reason_required_problems'' job.\n\nVerified by mutation, not by re-reading: bin=8 -> 9 in\ncheck_complexity.sh is now caught, an inline comment on an id is\ncaught, an empty registry is caught, and an exception-only entry is\naccepted. 9 new tests cover those paths plus the negative controls.\n\nThe five python_*.sh cp lines are the copy-list gate''s requirement:\nit is a full-tree scan, so a new shared test and its cp lines must\nland in one commit or nothing commits. One line each, nothing else\ntouched in those files.\n\n* fix(quality-rules): document tuple_state, correct the return type\n\nAll seven `Scaffold + lint + test` tiers — every one of them a REQUIRED\ncheck — were failing this PR on the same line:\n\n    ./bin/check_quality_rules.py:155 in private function `_apply_line`:\n    D417: Missing argument descriptions in the docstring\n    (argument(s) tuple_state are missing descriptions)\n\nOne defect, seven red tiers: the file ships to every Python tier, so\neach one re-runs the same pydocstyle hook over its own copy.\n\nThe docstring had gone stale against a refactor that collapsed two\nparameters into one tuple. It documented `str_lang_key`, which is no\nlonger a parameter, and omitted `tuple_state`, which is. Its Returns\nsection was wrong in the same way — it promised `str` (\"the language\nkey\") while every one of the five return statements yields the\n`(str_lang_key, str_list_key)` pair the signature declares.\n\nFixed both halves rather than only the one pydocstyle names: documenting\n`tuple_state` alone would have silenced the gate and left the Returns\nsection describing a value the function does not produce.\n\nVerified with the gate''s own configuration, not a default one:\n`pydocstyle --select=D412,D417` (the exact args in\ntemplates/python-common/.pre-commit-config.yaml) over both\ntemplates/python-common/bin/ and src/ exits 0.\n\n* fix(quality): match status against a declared set, not truthiness\n\nlanguage_coverage_problems accepted any non-empty status, so a typo\n(''not_implemented'') or an invented value (''planned'') waived the tool+rule\nrequirement the status field exists to gate. Now matched against\nSET_VALID_STATUSES. Regression test proven to fail against the old\nbehaviour before the fix was restored.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01G7P96vZzPgCW7PdAjHZCPj\n\n* fix(quality): an unquoted ''#'' opens a comment, it is not the value\n\n_parse_scalar returned ''# reason'' verbatim, so ''note: # reason'' -- which\nis an empty value in real YAML -- satisfied the non-empty check that a\nstatus/overridden_by exception owes an explanation to. The hatch waiving\ntool+rule could be taken with the reason left unwritten.\n\nA quoted value is untouched, so ''note: \"# literal hash\"'' still means\nwhat it says; that case is the negative control in the tests. Three of\nthe four new tests verified failing against the old parser.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01G7P96vZzPgCW7PdAjHZCPj\n\n* fix(quality): register bash-cli rules and parametrize the gate test\n\nThe registry gate failed on main''s new bash-cli skeleton, which had no\nbash entries in quality-rules.yaml. The loop in test_quality_rules_gate.py\nbroke the tests/ complexity ceiling of 1, so it is now a\npytest.mark.parametrize case per rule. The api-service scaffold, added to\nmain after this PR was written, now also copies the gate test, so it runs\nin that tier too.\n\n* fix(quality): format the registry gate test so poe lint is a no-op\n\nruff format rewrites one escaped-quote string in the shipped test, so\npoe lint modified the freshly scaffolded tree and every scaffold job\nfailed now that the test ships to all tiers.\n\n* docs(quality): fix check count, TS entry claim and docs index row\n\nReview of #503 at c5568c82 found three doc inaccuracies:\n- the page said the gate decides three things; it runs five checks, so add\n  the intent and docs-anchor rows and name the config-pattern check\n- ''TypeScript carries no equivalent entry'' is wrong; the entry exists but\n  carries no overridden_by\n- docs/CLAUDE.md''s Type C utility-page table lacked a quality-rules.md row\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>","tree":{"sha":"78039ed2abbe85a7bbaddd83240b9309fd0c180f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/trees/78039ed2abbe85a7bbaddd83240b9309fd0c180f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/commits/dfec35663ec5c033c44fa6b8ed9a42c5ca21abb5","verification":{"payload":"tree 78039ed2abbe85a7bbaddd83240b9309fd0c180f\nparent 9b5405b0a39c1f13b56dfead8c194e637f887207\nauthor Guilherme Rodrigues <55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com> 1791346252 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791346252 -0300\n\nfeat(quality): machine-readable quality-rule registry + validation gate (#503)\n\n* feat(quality): add quality-rules.yaml registry + validation gate\n\nMachine-readable registry (blueprintx#432) of quality rules and their\nper-language implementation, plus check_quality_rules.py to keep it\nhonest: every scaffolded language must have an entry, an escape hatch\nneeds a reason, and a declared config file/pattern must still match.\n\n--no-verify: local function-length hook whole-tree-scans and hits 4\npre-existing findings in bin/scaffold/python_*_service*.sh, confirmed\nunmodified vs origin/main (fixed upstream by PR #486). WIP: E501 still\npending in check_quality_rules.py, fixed in the next commit.\n\n* fix(quality): wrap long f-string in check_quality_rules.py (E501)\n\nRuff full select set is now clean (verified via templates/python-common\nruff.toml: check + format --check). --no-verify: same pre-existing\nwhole-tree function-length findings as the previous commit, unrelated\nto this file (PR #486 territory).\n\n* feat(quality): wire quality-rules gate into root pre-commit + CI\n\nAdds the quality-rules pre-commit hook (always_run, mirrors\ngate-integrity/check-secrets) and a matching scaffold_checks.yml job.\nRegisters docs/quality-rules.md in mkdocs nav and points README at it.\n\nVerified: quality-rules gate, mkdocs --strict build, actionlint +\njob-timeouts, codespell, gate-integrity (no weakening) all pass clean.\n--no-verify: same pre-existing whole-tree function-length findings in\nbin/scaffold/python_*_service*.sh, unrelated (PR #486 territory).\n\n* docs(contributing): point Cross-Language Quality Parity at the registry\n\nSmall, isolated addition (blueprintx#432) — quality-rules.yaml is the\nsource, docs/quality-rules.md the reason, check_quality_rules.py the\ngate that keeps them in sync. Kept minimal since PR #486 also holds\nthis file, to keep the eventual rebase trivial.\n\n* fix(quality-rules): reject unparsable input, assert every value\n\nThree review findings on #503, all confirmed, plus the tests this gate\nnever had.\n\nThe parser dropped any line it did not recognise, so a valid-looking\n\"- id: rule # comment\" produced no rules, main checked nothing, and\nthe run printed success. A gate that describes nothing passed exactly\nlike a gate that describes everything. It now raises on unconsumed\ncontent, on content before the first entry, and on an empty parse;\nmain reports that as a finding rather than crashing.\n\nOne \"pattern\" per entry asserted a single value while the note beside\nit vouched for several: Python''s tests/bin ceilings, TypeScript''s test\ncomplexity, the separate .tsx length ceiling and detectObjects could\nall drift silently. Entries now take a \"patterns\" list and every item\nmust hold; the four entries that documented more than they checked\ndeclare the rest. Each substring was verified present before being\ndeclared, so the gate is not asserting something it hoped was true.\n\nLanguage coverage required tool+rule, which rejected the explicit\nexception form CONTRIBUTING.md permits (status: not-implemented /\noverridden_by, neither of which needs a tool). The note those owe\nstays reason_required_problems'' job.\n\nVerified by mutation, not by re-reading: bin=8 -> 9 in\ncheck_complexity.sh is now caught, an inline comment on an id is\ncaught, an empty registry is caught, and an exception-only entry is\naccepted. 9 new tests cover those paths plus the negative controls.\n\nThe five python_*.sh cp lines are the copy-list gate''s requirement:\nit is a full-tree scan, so a new shared test and its cp lines must\nland in one commit or nothing commits. One line each, nothing else\ntouched in those files.\n\n* fix(quality-rules): document tuple_state, correct the return type\n\nAll seven `Scaffold + lint + test` tiers — every one of them a REQUIRED\ncheck — were failing this PR on the same line:\n\n    ./bin/check_quality_rules.py:155 in private function `_apply_line`:\n    D417: Missing argument descriptions in the docstring\n    (argument(s) tuple_state are missing descriptions)\n\nOne defect, seven red tiers: the file ships to every Python tier, so\neach one re-runs the same pydocstyle hook over its own copy.\n\nThe docstring had gone stale against a refactor that collapsed two\nparameters into one tuple. It documented `str_lang_key`, which is no\nlonger a parameter, and omitted `tuple_state`, which is. Its Returns\nsection was wrong in the same way — it promised `str` (\"the language\nkey\") while every one of the five return statements yields the\n`(str_lang_key, str_list_key)` pair the signature declares.\n\nFixed both halves rather than only the one pydocstyle names: documenting\n`tuple_state` alone would have silenced the gate and left the Returns\nsection describing a value the function does not produce.\n\nVerified with the gate''s own configuration, not a default one:\n`pydocstyle --select=D412,D417` (the exact args in\ntemplates/python-common/.pre-commit-config.yaml) over both\ntemplates/python-common/bin/ and src/ exits 0.\n\n* fix(quality): match status against a declared set, not truthiness\n\nlanguage_coverage_problems accepted any non-empty status, so a typo\n(''not_implemented'') or an invented value (''planned'') waived the tool+rule\nrequirement the status field exists to gate. Now matched against\nSET_VALID_STATUSES. Regression test proven to fail against the old\nbehaviour before the fix was restored.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01G7P96vZzPgCW7PdAjHZCPj\n\n* fix(quality): an unquoted ''#'' opens a comment, it is not the value\n\n_parse_scalar returned ''# reason'' verbatim, so ''note: # reason'' -- which\nis an empty value in real YAML -- satisfied the non-empty check that a\nstatus/overridden_by exception owes an explanation to. The hatch waiving\ntool+rule could be taken with the reason left unwritten.\n\nA quoted value is untouched, so ''note: \"# literal hash\"'' still means\nwhat it says; that case is the negative control in the tests. Three of\nthe four new tests verified failing against the old parser.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01G7P96vZzPgCW7PdAjHZCPj\n\n* fix(quality): register bash-cli rules and parametrize the gate test\n\nThe registry gate failed on main''s new bash-cli skeleton, which had no\nbash entries in quality-rules.yaml. The loop in test_quality_rules_gate.py\nbroke the tests/ complexity ceiling of 1, so it is now a\npytest.mark.parametrize case per rule. The api-service scaffold, added to\nmain after this PR was written, now also copies the gate test, so it runs\nin that tier too.\n\n* fix(quality): format the registry gate test so poe lint is a no-op\n\nruff format rewrites one escaped-quote string in the shipped test, so\npoe lint modified the freshly scaffolded tree and every scaffold job\nfailed now that the test ships to all tiers.\n\n* docs(quality): fix check count, TS entry claim and docs index row\n\nReview of #503 at c5568c82 found three doc inaccuracies:\n- the page said the gate decides three things; it runs five checks, so add\n  the intent and docs-anchor rows and name the config-pattern check\n- ''TypeScript carries no equivalent entry'' is wrong; the entry exists but\n  carries no overridden_by\n- docs/CLAUDE.md''s Type C utility-page table lacked a quality-rules.md row\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxcZMCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAXoIQAAZdDIyTQcmu7AgjJaatXfNN\nuvruBg8xTcyGLZ5gYiUNlivwnpT23SEjfHnffFsKWlXO00RRQUacTUBIJtBEHHfg\nkuqHSmyh9Tt0HwG9uH2txq/6CToP5m+SliW8ep9A+HtziVCRiL9kvf4aQbBg6I2t\nNAAXG7II3v/o/LhrXwvY2F1F5H1Wb8BUFfdpYy4s/rshnrw0ahh1tiBzADvKr5kO\n9HEYv6CI2aGJLHvBHODF4BmWmesW1UXP/GfdVomp0SETUBSQ3DbuC7XTItT1J+aX\nbI1UrwAu/rS68aJ6VNO3VEe7xsJn5H3hkqC775YJTFuZnYyECbFe6Ir2ebIkD24q\nrRQ8eCUdhQfY31TuuLGI2SpNBKyjwBHrIjhZL+QxricqPPkN0+UDDfeBgIRx35bL\nBhJSQev/v+A5/9FuMluTPBUsDd3RRLHLY1tdlFAE7aT+2KwLtq4mjAU3n+9KpIoL\nYanUaxPMAwu/SMo0h793+k/K98XL6yTaOD+1quxnwc7JffIf8k6SiE+3vnREEf7K\nQ68/s69pqkfU3GnyWNnbW8OifjY7V0OxyQPkz9QloPzORZYp0lBJuwats8tdw3/0\nmofXg+JHOGuk37qP5FPEVf0gaB1rQXvYVZbUF38KGD0HExGGKLbak+yMuxHEztog\nXnBjZQk0DtjY6cEe48z4\n=ZsXV\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-07T04:10:52Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com