in":"guilhermegor","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MDUzMTg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/39067498efb4708859843448447773a8a6154f57/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T23:22:14Z","email":"55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Guilherme Rodrigues"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T23:22:14Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(ci): cap unit tests at one assertion site, and clear 79 (#544) (#581)\n\n* docs(tests): a unit test asserts exactly once (#544)\n\nRewrites the tracked rule in templates/python-common/tests/CLAUDE.md, which\nsaid the opposite (\"One BEHAVIOUR per test - not one assert\"). Until it\nchanged, the doc outranked the decision and the next session was right to\nre-derive the old rule from the repo.\n\nThe new rule is a split, not a narrowing:\n\n- tests/unit/    exactly ONE assertion site, enforced and blocking.\n- tests/integration/  one BEHAVIOUR, several asserts allowed - the old\n  argument survives where it is still correct, as the reason that half\n  stays loose.\n\nWhy one assert in a unit test: two asserts that go red do not say which\nbehaviour broke, and the green never says which one RAN - an assert after\nan early return/raise/raises block is never reached and is reported as\nproven anyway.\n\nRe-measured 2026-09-20 (AST assertion-site count over templates/**, the\nsame counter the gate uses):\n\n  tests/unit/         828 tests, 1 zero, 626 exactly one, 201 (24%) over\n  tests/integration/   68 tests, 0 zero,  16 exactly one,  52 (76%) over\n\nAt 24% the unit rule is finishable; at 76% the same rule on integration\ntests is a wall, and a gate nobody pays is a gate nobody keeps. Supersedes\n#429''s 554/31% and #431''s 188/33%, both stale.\n\nAlso removes the two restatements of the old rule that would have\nreinstated it (the check_complexity row in python-common/CLAUDE.md and the\nsame sentence copied into .pre-commit-config.yaml), and wires the\none-assert pre-commit hook.\n\n* feat(ci): cap tests/unit at one assertion site (#544)\n\nBuilds on PR #543''s gate rather than adding a second one - the file is\nchecked out from that branch and changed in place, so the one-implementation\nrule holds whichever lands first. #543 is NOT on main yet; if it merges\nfirst, this rebases onto it as the small default-flip diff it is.\n\nWhat changed on top of #543:\n\n- The cap is on by DEFAULT and blocking (DEFAULT_UNIT_MAX = 1), where #543\n  shipped it as an unwired opt-in --max-per-test flag.\n- Scope is by PATH, never by filename: --unit-dir (default tests/unit)\n  decides which files are capped. A test_x.py under tests/integration/ is\n  an integration test and keeps the loose rule.\n- --max-per-test overrides the cap; --max-per-test 0 disables it and leaves\n  the zero-assertion check alone. 0 cannot mean ''cap at zero'' - a test with\n  zero sites is already the other, unconditional finding.\n- The finding names the remedy (parametrize / scoped fixture), not just the\n  count. A finding that does not say how to fix it is one people route\n  around.\n- parse_args split into a flag table + a resolver: a third flag as another\n  elif would have pushed this file past bin/''s complexity ceiling of 8.\n\nShould-fail witness, both directions, on a throwaway tree:\n\n  tests/unit/test_two.py (2 asserts)          -> exit 1, names file+count\n  tests/unit/test_one.py (1 assert)           -> exit 0\n  tests/integration/test_seq.py (3 asserts)   -> exit 0\n\nThe gate''s own suite is rewritten to the rule it enforces: 38 tests, one\nassertion site each, the multi-claim cases carried by parametrize rather\nthan by extra asserts.\n\n* chore(ci): hold the one-assert hook at stages:[manual] (#544)\n\nThe hook is declared here so the wiring is reviewed with the gate, but it\ndoes not run yet. Measured on this branch with the shipped gate as the\ncounter:\n\n  templates/python-common      183 findings / 858 tests\n  templates/mvc-service-native-db  8 findings /  32 tests\n  templates/mvc-service-orm-db     8 findings /  21 tests\n\nA gate that lands on 199 known violations is red on its first run, and a\ngate that is red on its first run gets turned off rather than kept - this\nrepo has already measured that shape. So the hook and the LAST batch of\nsplits land in the same PR. There is no warning phase and no\ngrandfathering: no version of this tree ever ships the rule unenforced\nWITH violations still in it, which is the outcome the owner''s decision\nasked for.\n\n* test(layers): one assert per unit test in the layer-import gate (#544)\n\n13 violations to 0, 31 passing (was 26). Two patterns, no mechanical\nduplication of the arrange:\n\n- parametrize, where the asserts were the SAME assertion over different\n  fragments of one captured output (the message must name the vendor AND\n  the evasion; the finding AND its remedy). One function, N named cases.\n- two precondition helpers, where the claims were of different kinds:\n  _sole() takes the ''exactly one finding, not a cascade'' claim and\n  _output_at() takes the exit code. Both are preconditions of the\n  behaviour under test - an output assertion over a run that crashed\n  proves nothing - so they belong beside the arrange, not beside the\n  behaviour.\n\n⚠️ Hoisting those STRENGTHENS the suite rather than dropping asserts:\nseveral tests previously indexed list_problems[0] without checking the\ncount at all and would have passed on a cascade. Every caller now checks\nit. The failed assertion carries the whole output as its message, so a\nwrong exit code still reports what the gate actually printed.\n\nNo test gained a branch - the complexity-1 cap on tests/ is the same\nargument one level up, and parametrize adds cases without adding paths.\n\n* test(ledger): one assert per unit test in the backlog-ledger gate (#544)\n\n12 violations to 0, 78 passing (was 67). Mostly parametrize - these were\nthe textbook case, one assertion applied to a list of inputs (three bot\nlogins, three routine path classes, two bare-marker spellings, three\nwatched/unwatched paths). Each case is now named in the failure output\ninstead of the reader counting asserts to find which input broke.\n\nThree needed a real split rather than a parametrize, and each is\njustified in place:\n\n- classify_risk-collapses vs needs_ledger-catches-it: two different\n  functions, not one function over two inputs. Split, sharing the branch\n  as a module constant.\n- ''a missing event file exempts nobody'' vs ''...resolves to no author at\n  all'': the behaviour and the mechanism behind it. Keeping only the first\n  would let a resolver returning some other non-bot login pass while the\n  real defect - falling back to GITHUB_ACTOR - went unnamed.\n- the dispatch table and the workflow-path regex are two independent\n  doors; one assertion over both cannot say which opened.\n\n⚠️ No assertion was dropped. Where a count claim (''exactly one finding,\nnot a cascade'') sat beside a content claim, it became its own test - and\nin the assertion-weakening case that test is what keeps the\n''does-not-name-the-innocent-test'' case meaningful, since that case now\nreads only the first problem. _first_problem() takes the non-empty\nprecondition so an IndexError can never stand in for a real failure.\n\n* test(threads): one assert per unit test in the review-thread gate (#544)\n\n10 violations to 0, 96 passing (was 85). This file needed all three\npatterns, which makes it the worked example for the rule:\n\n1. A MODULE-SCOPED FIXTURE for the pagination case. One two-page fetch\n   backed five independent claims; splitting it into five tests that each\n   re-run the fetch is precisely the mechanical split tests/CLAUDE.md\n   warns about, so the fetch happens once and the five tests read its\n   immutable result. ⚠️ pytest.MonkeyPatch.context() inside the fixture -\n   the monkeypatch fixture is function-scoped and cannot be requested\n   from a module-scoped one.\n2. PARAMETRIZE where it was one assertion over inputs: the three login\n   spellings normalise_login must handle, and the two halves of the\n   PENDING-filter claim.\n3. A REAL SPLIT where the claims were different verdicts, each with the\n   reason written at the split:\n   - roster read vs roster APPLIED: a gate that parses a swapped-in\n     reviewer but never polices it passes the first claim alone.\n   - roster absent-and-never-adopted vs absent-but-upstream: opposite\n     verdicts over the same input.\n   - the resolve flag''s three claims. The third - ''switching the resolve\n     half off does not disable the reply half'' - is the one a\n     three-assert test hides: the first two say the flag changes\n     something, only the third says it did not change everything.\n\n_sole() and _reported() take the count and not-None preconditions, so an\nIndexError or a TypeError on None can never stand in for a real failure.\n\n* test(gates): one assert per unit test in three more gates (#544)\n\ncheck_assertion_weakening, check_comment_language, check_pr_gate:\n24 violations to 0, 109 passing (was 85). Running total 148 -> 124.\n\nOverwhelmingly parametrize, because these three files are where the\n''same assertion over different inputs'' shape is densest - three login\nspellings, three trivialised assertions, three risk collapses, three\nlockfile shapes, three gate states, four rendered-comment fragments.\nEach input is now named in the failure output instead of the reader\ncounting asserts to find which one broke.\n\n_sole_finding() / _sole() carry the ''exactly one finding'' precondition\nin the two gate files whose every fixture is a two-line diff built to\ntrip exactly one rule.\n\nFive needed a real split, each because the two claims were different\nkinds and one could pass while the other silently did not:\n\n- classify_risk labels a path ''other'' / that class is not auto-mergeable\n  (a rename keeps one green).\n- an axis goes red / the failing-check NAMES are recorded (the map can\n  be empty while the axis is red - the regression worth its own name).\n- consecutive comment lines JOIN into one block / the joined block is\n  not charged the quotation''s language (without the join, the second\n  passes for the wrong reason).\n- the named unaudited locations / the open-ended tracked-file property:\n  the file''s own docstring says these are deliberately different kinds\n  of claim, one non-tautological and one open-ended.\n- ''docs'' is declared in the skip list / no docs file is audited (an\n  empty discovery satisfies the second alone).\n\n* test(utils): one assert per unit test in paths and frames (#544)\n\n12 violations to 0. These two are the FIXTURE half of the split pattern,\nwhere the previous files were mostly parametrize: several claims about\none built artifact, which is exactly the case tests/CLAUDE.md''s\n''Expensive shared setup'' section was written for.\n\n- test_frames: four module-scoped fixtures build the cursor-shaped,\n  record-shaped, empty and date-column frames ONCE, and the tests read\n  them. The cursor and record seams owe the same three properties, so\n  those are parametrized over the two fixture NAMES via\n  request.getfixturevalue - one case list, both seams, no duplicated\n  arrange.\n- test_paths: function-scoped fixtures for copy_into and resolve_input\n  (they write to tmp_path, so module scope would leak between tests),\n  plus a fixture for the simulated-Windows to_absolute call.\n\nClaims kept that a mechanical split would have lost:\n\n- ''ensure_dir creates it'' and ''ensure_dir RETURNS it'' - a helper that\n  returned None passes the first and breaks every chaining caller.\n- ''copy_into names the destination'' and ''copy_into copies the bytes'' -\n  computing the stamped path without copying passes the first.\n- ''map_with_default leaks no NaN'' and ''the unmapped value becomes the\n  DEFAULT'' - dropping the row satisfies the first.\n- the four driveless-path claims: the file''s own docstring already\n  argued the premise and the behaviour must not collapse into one test,\n  and now they cannot.\n\n⚠️ ONE assertion was deliberately folded rather than split, and it is\nnamed in the docstring: to_absolute''s is_absolute() check, because the\nequality it sits beside compares against a resolve()-d path, so a\nseparate test could not fail independently.\n\n⚠️ Neither file collects at the template root - src/utils/*.py import\nchassis.typing, which is injected at scaffold time. Verified\nPRE-EXISTING by stashing the edit and re-running: same\nModuleNotFoundError on the unmodified file. Both compile, and\nbin/ci/scaffold_lint_test.sh is where they actually run.\n\n* test: clear the eight single-violation unit files (#544)\n\n8 violations to 0; 124 -> 104 across python-common. Small files, but\nthree of them needed a decision rather than a mechanical rewrite, and\nthe reason is written at each site:\n\n- test_pip_requirements: ''pytest.raises'' IS the exit assertion, so the\n  message check moves into its match= instead of being a second assert\n  inside the block. Same two checks, one site.\n- test_ms_office_outlook_gateway: is_absolute() + .name folded into one\n  equality against the resolved path. STRICTLY STRONGER - it pins WHERE\n  the attachment was anchored, not merely that it was anchored at all,\n  which is the actual defect (a foreign process re-anchoring a relative\n  path).\n- test_family_convention_example: ''the family is not empty'' split from\n  ''the family matches the declared surface''. A broken discovery and a\n  drifted __all__ are different failures with different causes, and this\n  file is the shipped TEMPLATE for the introspective-convention pattern\n  - it has to demonstrate the rule, not just satisfy it.\n\nThe rest are parametrize over message fragments, with a dict mapping the\ncase id to its expected fragment where the two fragments are of\ndifferent kinds (a file:line and a source line).\n\n⚠️ test_ms_office_excel_sheet_names, test_email_html_body and\ntest_ms_office_outlook_gateway do not collect at the template root\n(src/utils/*.py import chassis.typing, injected at scaffold time) -\npre-existing, verified on the unmodified tree. They compile; they run in\na generated project.\n\n* fix(ci): clear ruff findings, wire one-assert test copy, fix RE_HATCH\n\nSeven ruff findings across templates/python-common/bin/check_one_assert.py\nand its companion tests/unit/*.py were red on every Scaffold+lint+test\ntier: an E501-long docstring line, an ERA001 false positive triggered by a\ncomment ending in a colon, two missing `import pytest` (F821), and two more\nE501 lines. Reworded/split each to stay under 99 chars while keeping the\nsame meaning, and fixed test_pip_requirements.py''s comment to describe the\ncode that is actually there (no `assert` inside the `with` block).\n\ntemplates/python-common/tests/unit/test_one_assert_gate.py had no `cp` line\nin any of the six bin/scaffold/python_*.sh scripts, so it would ship in\npython-common but never run in a generated project — caught by\n`bin/ci/check_test_copy_lists.py`. Added the companion cp line next to the\nexisting check_fixture_scope.py one in all six scaffolds.\n\ntemplates/ts-common/package.json declares eslint-plugin-jest ^29.0.0 but\npackage-lock.json had no entry for it, so `npm ci` failed before reaching\ntsc in the TypeScript typecheck job. Regenerated the lockfile with\n`npm install --package-lock-only`.\n\nRE_HATCH''s `\\s*` after `one-assert-ok:` matched a newline, so a bare\nmarker on any line but the last let the reason group borrow the next\nline of code, silently exempting a test the rule means to catch. Narrowed\nto `[ \\t]*` and added a regression test that pins the failure.\n\nRef: blueprintx#544\n\n* test(gate): pin the unittest-to-bare-assert message, not a bare ''in''\n\n''in'' is a substring of almost any finding text, so the old assertion passed\non any message at all. Match the phrase the gate actually emits.\n\n* fix(tests): record why the split fixtures are module-scoped\n\nThe fixture-scope gate failed three tiers on six module-scoped\nfixtures introduced by the one-assert splits. Each frame or fetch\nresult is read-only, so the reason goes on the decorator line.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tg7pRKKVCMeVgKKNtbue5G\n\n* style(tests): apply ruff format to three split test files\n\nThe lint step of every tier failed on format drift left by the\none-assert splits; formatting only, no behaviour change.\n\n* fix(tests): match docstring types to hints on frame fixtures\n\ncheck-docstrings failed the mvc-service-orm-db tier: the fixtures\nreturn object/tuple because pandas is imported lazily, but the\ndocstrings said pandas.DataFrame. Align the types, keep the prose.\n\n* fix(gate): count each assertion context, honor hatches only in comments\n\nReview findings on check_one_assert.py: a with statement holding both\npytest.raises and pytest.warns counted as one site, and a hatch marker\ninside a string literal exempted the test. Both now fail the cap, each\nwith a regression test. Also span the quotation fixture over both\ncomment lines so the redaction test exercises the multi-line case.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FSNxnx3xHEyT49GBNg6XF6\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>","tree":{"sha":"a4e683508989fa11254a461c4ca3cfeaff84e3db","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/trees/a4e683508989fa11254a461c4ca3cfeaff84e3db"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/commits/39067498efb4708859843448447773a8a6154f57","verification":{"payload":"tree a4e683508989fa11254a461c4ca3cfeaff84e3db\nparent f85ddc3e929a49b5a694e4d438fd38522eb4835f\nauthor Guilherme Rodrigues <55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com> 1791328934 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791328934 -0300\n\nfeat(ci): cap unit tests at one assertion site, and clear 79 (#544) (#581)\n\n* docs(tests): a unit test asserts exactly once (#544)\n\nRewrites the tracked rule in templates/python-common/tests/CLAUDE.md, which\nsaid the opposite (\"One BEHAVIOUR per test - not one assert\"). Until it\nchanged, the doc outranked the decision and the next session was right to\nre-derive the old rule from the repo.\n\nThe new rule is a split, not a narrowing:\n\n- tests/unit/    exactly ONE assertion site, enforced and blocking.\n- tests/integration/  one BEHAVIOUR, several asserts allowed - the old\n  argument survives where it is still correct, as the reason that half\n  stays loose.\n\nWhy one assert in a unit test: two asserts that go red do not say which\nbehaviour broke, and the green never says which one RAN - an assert after\nan early return/raise/raises block is never reached and is reported as\nproven anyway.\n\nRe-measured 2026-09-20 (AST assertion-site count over templates/**, the\nsame counter the gate uses):\n\n  tests/unit/         828 tests, 1 zero, 626 exactly one, 201 (24%) over\n  tests/integration/   68 tests, 0 zero,  16 exactly one,  52 (76%) over\n\nAt 24% the unit rule is finishable; at 76% the same rule on integration\ntests is a wall, and a gate nobody pays is a gate nobody keeps. Supersedes\n#429''s 554/31% and #431''s 188/33%, both stale.\n\nAlso removes the two restatements of the old rule that would have\nreinstated it (the check_complexity row in python-common/CLAUDE.md and the\nsame sentence copied into .pre-commit-config.yaml), and wires the\none-assert pre-commit hook.\n\n* feat(ci): cap tests/unit at one assertion site (#544)\n\nBuilds on PR #543''s gate rather than adding a second one - the file is\nchecked out from that branch and changed in place, so the one-implementation\nrule holds whichever lands first. #543 is NOT on main yet; if it merges\nfirst, this rebases onto it as the small default-flip diff it is.\n\nWhat changed on top of #543:\n\n- The cap is on by DEFAULT and blocking (DEFAULT_UNIT_MAX = 1), where #543\n  shipped it as an unwired opt-in --max-per-test flag.\n- Scope is by PATH, never by filename: --unit-dir (default tests/unit)\n  decides which files are capped. A test_x.py under tests/integration/ is\n  an integration test and keeps the loose rule.\n- --max-per-test overrides the cap; --max-per-test 0 disables it and leaves\n  the zero-assertion check alone. 0 cannot mean ''cap at zero'' - a test with\n  zero sites is already the other, unconditional finding.\n- The finding names the remedy (parametrize / scoped fixture), not just the\n  count. A finding that does not say how to fix it is one people route\n  around.\n- parse_args split into a flag table + a resolver: a third flag as another\n  elif would have pushed this file past bin/''s complexity ceiling of 8.\n\nShould-fail witness, both directions, on a throwaway tree:\n\n  tests/unit/test_two.py (2 asserts)          -> exit 1, names file+count\n  tests/unit/test_one.py (1 assert)           -> exit 0\n  tests/integration/test_seq.py (3 asserts)   -> exit 0\n\nThe gate''s own suite is rewritten to the rule it enforces: 38 tests, one\nassertion site each, the multi-claim cases carried by parametrize rather\nthan by extra asserts.\n\n* chore(ci): hold the one-assert hook at stages:[manual] (#544)\n\nThe hook is declared here so the wiring is reviewed with the gate, but it\ndoes not run yet. Measured on this branch with the shipped gate as the\ncounter:\n\n  templates/python-common      183 findings / 858 tests\n  templates/mvc-service-native-db  8 findings /  32 tests\n  templates/mvc-service-orm-db     8 findings /  21 tests\n\nA gate that lands on 199 known violations is red on its first run, and a\ngate that is red on its first run gets turned off rather than kept - this\nrepo has already measured that shape. So the hook and the LAST batch of\nsplits land in the same PR. There is no warning phase and no\ngrandfathering: no version of this tree ever ships the rule unenforced\nWITH violations still in it, which is the outcome the owner''s decision\nasked for.\n\n* test(layers): one assert per unit test in the layer-import gate (#544)\n\n13 violations to 0, 31 passing (was 26). Two patterns, no mechanical\nduplication of the arrange:\n\n- parametrize, where the asserts were the SAME assertion over different\n  fragments of one captured output (the message must name the vendor AND\n  the evasion; the finding AND its remedy). One function, N named cases.\n- two precondition helpers, where the claims were of different kinds:\n  _sole() takes the ''exactly one finding, not a cascade'' claim and\n  _output_at() takes the exit code. Both are preconditions of the\n  behaviour under test - an output assertion over a run that crashed\n  proves nothing - so they belong beside the arrange, not beside the\n  behaviour.\n\n⚠️ Hoisting those STRENGTHENS the suite rather than dropping asserts:\nseveral tests previously indexed list_problems[0] without checking the\ncount at all and would have passed on a cascade. Every caller now checks\nit. The failed assertion carries the whole output as its message, so a\nwrong exit code still reports what the gate actually printed.\n\nNo test gained a branch - the complexity-1 cap on tests/ is the same\nargument one level up, and parametrize adds cases without adding paths.\n\n* test(ledger): one assert per unit test in the backlog-ledger gate (#544)\n\n12 violations to 0, 78 passing (was 67). Mostly parametrize - these were\nthe textbook case, one assertion applied to a list of inputs (three bot\nlogins, three routine path classes, two bare-marker spellings, three\nwatched/unwatched paths). Each case is now named in the failure output\ninstead of the reader counting asserts to find which input broke.\n\nThree needed a real split rather than a parametrize, and each is\njustified in place:\n\n- classify_risk-collapses vs needs_ledger-catches-it: two different\n  functions, not one function over two inputs. Split, sharing the branch\n  as a module constant.\n- ''a missing event file exempts nobody'' vs ''...resolves to no author at\n  all'': the behaviour and the mechanism behind it. Keeping only the first\n  would let a resolver returning some other non-bot login pass while the\n  real defect - falling back to GITHUB_ACTOR - went unnamed.\n- the dispatch table and the workflow-path regex are two independent\n  doors; one assertion over both cannot say which opened.\n\n⚠️ No assertion was dropped. Where a count claim (''exactly one finding,\nnot a cascade'') sat beside a content claim, it became its own test - and\nin the assertion-weakening case that test is what keeps the\n''does-not-name-the-innocent-test'' case meaningful, since that case now\nreads only the first problem. _first_problem() takes the non-empty\nprecondition so an IndexError can never stand in for a real failure.\n\n* test(threads): one assert per unit test in the review-thread gate (#544)\n\n10 violations to 0, 96 passing (was 85). This file needed all three\npatterns, which makes it the worked example for the rule:\n\n1. A MODULE-SCOPED FIXTURE for the pagination case. One two-page fetch\n   backed five independent claims; splitting it into five tests that each\n   re-run the fetch is precisely the mechanical split tests/CLAUDE.md\n   warns about, so the fetch happens once and the five tests read its\n   immutable result. ⚠️ pytest.MonkeyPatch.context() inside the fixture -\n   the monkeypatch fixture is function-scoped and cannot be requested\n   from a module-scoped one.\n2. PARAMETRIZE where it was one assertion over inputs: the three login\n   spellings normalise_login must handle, and the two halves of the\n   PENDING-filter claim.\n3. A REAL SPLIT where the claims were different verdicts, each with the\n   reason written at the split:\n   - roster read vs roster APPLIED: a gate that parses a swapped-in\n     reviewer but never polices it passes the first claim alone.\n   - roster absent-and-never-adopted vs absent-but-upstream: opposite\n     verdicts over the same input.\n   - the resolve flag''s three claims. The third - ''switching the resolve\n     half off does not disable the reply half'' - is the one a\n     three-assert test hides: the first two say the flag changes\n     something, only the third says it did not change everything.\n\n_sole() and _reported() take the count and not-None preconditions, so an\nIndexError or a TypeError on None can never stand in for a real failure.\n\n* test(gates): one assert per unit test in three more gates (#544)\n\ncheck_assertion_weakening, check_comment_language, check_pr_gate:\n24 violations to 0, 109 passing (was 85). Running total 148 -> 124.\n\nOverwhelmingly parametrize, because these three files are where the\n''same assertion over different inputs'' shape is densest - three login\nspellings, three trivialised assertions, three risk collapses, three\nlockfile shapes, three gate states, four rendered-comment fragments.\nEach input is now named in the failure output instead of the reader\ncounting asserts to find which one broke.\n\n_sole_finding() / _sole() carry the ''exactly one finding'' precondition\nin the two gate files whose every fixture is a two-line diff built to\ntrip exactly one rule.\n\nFive needed a real split, each because the two claims were different\nkinds and one could pass while the other silently did not:\n\n- classify_risk labels a path ''other'' / that class is not auto-mergeable\n  (a rename keeps one green).\n- an axis goes red / the failing-check NAMES are recorded (the map can\n  be empty while the axis is red - the regression worth its own name).\n- consecutive comment lines JOIN into one block / the joined block is\n  not charged the quotation''s language (without the join, the second\n  passes for the wrong reason).\n- the named unaudited locations / the open-ended tracked-file property:\n  the file''s own docstring says these are deliberately different kinds\n  of claim, one non-tautological and one open-ended.\n- ''docs'' is declared in the skip list / no docs file is audited (an\n  empty discovery satisfies the second alone).\n\n* test(utils): one assert per unit test in paths and frames (#544)\n\n12 violations to 0. These two are the FIXTURE half of the split pattern,\nwhere the previous files were mostly parametrize: several claims about\none built artifact, which is exactly the case tests/CLAUDE.md''s\n''Expensive shared setup'' section was written for.\n\n- test_frames: four module-scoped fixtures build the cursor-shaped,\n  record-shaped, empty and date-column frames ONCE, and the tests read\n  them. The cursor and record seams owe the same three properties, so\n  those are parametrized over the two fixture NAMES via\n  request.getfixturevalue - one case list, both seams, no duplicated\n  arrange.\n- test_paths: function-scoped fixtures for copy_into and resolve_input\n  (they write to tmp_path, so module scope would leak between tests),\n  plus a fixture for the simulated-Windows to_absolute call.\n\nClaims kept that a mechanical split would have lost:\n\n- ''ensure_dir creates it'' and ''ensure_dir RETURNS it'' - a helper that\n  returned None passes the first and breaks every chaining caller.\n- ''copy_into names the destination'' and ''copy_into copies the bytes'' -\n  computing the stamped path without copying passes the first.\n- ''map_with_default leaks no NaN'' and ''the unmapped value becomes the\n  DEFAULT'' - dropping the row satisfies the first.\n- the four driveless-path claims: the file''s own docstring already\n  argued the premise and the behaviour must not collapse into one test,\n  and now they cannot.\n\n⚠️ ONE assertion was deliberately folded rather than split, and it is\nnamed in the docstring: to_absolute''s is_absolute() check, because the\nequality it sits beside compares against a resolve()-d path, so a\nseparate test could not fail independently.\n\n⚠️ Neither file collects at the template root - src/utils/*.py import\nchassis.typing, which is injected at scaffold time. Verified\nPRE-EXISTING by stashing the edit and re-running: same\nModuleNotFoundError on the unmodified file. Both compile, and\nbin/ci/scaffold_lint_test.sh is where they actually run.\n\n* test: clear the eight single-violation unit files (#544)\n\n8 violations to 0; 124 -> 104 across python-common. Small files, but\nthree of them needed a decision rather than a mechanical rewrite, and\nthe reason is written at each site:\n\n- test_pip_requirements: ''pytest.raises'' IS the exit assertion, so the\n  message check moves into its match= instead of being a second assert\n  inside the block. Same two checks, one site.\n- test_ms_office_outlook_gateway: is_absolute() + .name folded into one\n  equality against the resolved path. STRICTLY STRONGER - it pins WHERE\n  the attachment was anchored, not merely that it was anchored at all,\n  which is the actual defect (a foreign process re-anchoring a relative\n  path).\n- test_family_convention_example: ''the family is not empty'' split from\n  ''the family matches the declared surface''. A broken discovery and a\n  drifted __all__ are different failures with different causes, and this\n  file is the shipped TEMPLATE for the introspective-convention pattern\n  - it has to demonstrate the rule, not just satisfy it.\n\nThe rest are parametrize over message fragments, with a dict mapping the\ncase id to its expected fragment where the two fragments are of\ndifferent kinds (a file:line and a source line).\n\n⚠️ test_ms_office_excel_sheet_names, test_email_html_body and\ntest_ms_office_outlook_gateway do not collect at the template root\n(src/utils/*.py import chassis.typing, injected at scaffold time) -\npre-existing, verified on the unmodified tree. They compile; they run in\na generated project.\n\n* fix(ci): clear ruff findings, wire one-assert test copy, fix RE_HATCH\n\nSeven ruff findings across templates/python-common/bin/check_one_assert.py\nand its companion tests/unit/*.py were red on every Scaffold+lint+test\ntier: an E501-long docstring line, an ERA001 false positive triggered by a\ncomment ending in a colon, two missing `import pytest` (F821), and two more\nE501 lines. Reworded/split each to stay under 99 chars while keeping the\nsame meaning, and fixed test_pip_requirements.py''s comment to describe the\ncode that is actually there (no `assert` inside the `with` block).\n\ntemplates/python-common/tests/unit/test_one_assert_gate.py had no `cp` line\nin any of the six bin/scaffold/python_*.sh scripts, so it would ship in\npython-common but never run in a generated project — caught by\n`bin/ci/check_test_copy_lists.py`. Added the companion cp line next to the\nexisting check_fixture_scope.py one in all six scaffolds.\n\ntemplates/ts-common/package.json declares eslint-plugin-jest ^29.0.0 but\npackage-lock.json had no entry for it, so `npm ci` failed before reaching\ntsc in the TypeScript typecheck job. Regenerated the lockfile with\n`npm install --package-lock-only`.\n\nRE_HATCH''s `\\s*` after `one-assert-ok:` matched a newline, so a bare\nmarker on any line but the last let the reason group borrow the next\nline of code, silently exempting a test the rule means to catch. Narrowed\nto `[ \\t]*` and added a regression test that pins the failure.\n\nRef: blueprintx#544\n\n* test(gate): pin the unittest-to-bare-assert message, not a bare ''in''\n\n''in'' is a substring of almost any finding text, so the old assertion passed\non any message at all. Match the phrase the gate actually emits.\n\n* fix(tests): record why the split fixtures are module-scoped\n\nThe fixture-scope gate failed three tiers on six module-scoped\nfixtures introduced by the one-assert splits. Each frame or fetch\nresult is read-only, so the reason goes on the decorator line.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tg7pRKKVCMeVgKKNtbue5G\n\n* style(tests): apply ruff format to three split test files\n\nThe lint step of every tier failed on format drift left by the\none-assert splits; formatting only, no behaviour change.\n\n* fix(tests): match docstring types to hints on frame fixtures\n\ncheck-docstrings failed the mvc-service-orm-db tier: the fixtures\nreturn object/tuple because pandas is imported lazily, but the\ndocstrings said pandas.DataFrame. Align the types, keep the prose.\n\n* fix(gate): count each assertion context, honor hatches only in comments\n\nReview findings on check_one_assert.py: a with statement holding both\npytest.raises and pytest.warns counted as one site, and a hatch marker\ninside a string literal exempted the test. Both now fail the cap, each\nwith a regression test. Also span the quotation fixture over both\ncomment lines so the redaction test exercises the multi-line case.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FSNxnx3xHEyT49GBNg6XF6\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxYKmCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAC3QQAGZXV5/ZCbm/IcsXj54UmHyt\nDtwv1o0upS+m2mO0XOKTbXYL489GenAJifvZIBOrKFeRkTo0oQxc6i/yFfqKa60Z\naumAPqk1+gUiQ0ygZyvN3P9MalMXHqBjTmlxV8C6Xxj7w/Hx/sq81JG4dRdJ+pY7\nqspHSKysitPRTgSqg6U278/YyKbBEgGPJJOAykKtcwXxavRoDOeQHV/Bs2hl/MlL\n090EwsmTqhf35IqvFCJGnCT7WosDLMeRIaEv8rtCgE1/RcA3mRGM6FDFN7EN4s2L\ns4Hi31PXnShwf5AYVfPBI6M/ZsEofMj8ygqK63eqThI367OCidgYBIqE6sr3rpE/\ng6zN6FTCvIC2kDLC9ptMGJbFG0lDjkZaRWYOeskiJuuaHbdnv5upEHe+2nt71X30\n0/IjUm8qqc7GiRMDJBPKqqko0ZzrxNeH7CYVP8wgiq0crx5XBCM9yZD1dFAWVzIR\nVpwhThZvGC2GlwukOf/V1dM/fkJrfcAfrutnTLacjfRqi8zVn3+6Z8JVWAVGhsts\n5Kkq4lRdRGfswP6ibKvTXgWrHtWtkGTMOlDhTElxi/nWlHymNvGEbHTx5fyA4QxI\nLo4wGmSG0F4pOGH+O6+ATKHIGG+bW4Wj3lgDqr3/cyF9yHplCQqL6MQX3lSMr481\nKbEWfl2geR9ZNjmIQXTY\n=hL7a\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T23:22:14Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commit/39067498efb4708859843448447773a8a6154f57","node_id":"C_kwDORFcNJtoAKDM5MDY3NDk4ZWZiNDcwODg1OTg0MzQ0ODQ0Nzc3M2E4YTYxNTRmNTc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commit/f85ddc3e929a49b5a694e4d438fd38522eb4835f","sha":"f85ddc3e929a49b5a694e4d438fd38522eb4835f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/f85ddc3e929a49b5a694e4d438fd38522eb4835f"}],"sha":"39067498efb4708859843448447773a8a6154f57","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/39067498efb4708859843448447773a8a6154f57"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55053188?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor","id":55053188,"login":"guilhermegor","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MDUzMTg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/f85ddc3e929a49b5a694e4d438fd38522eb4835f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T23:13:13Z","email":"55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Guilherme Rodrigues"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T23:13:13Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(templates): ship a published incident-record page in docs/ (#316)\n\n* feat(templates): ship a published incident-record page in docs/\n\nAdds templates/python-common/docs/incidents.md — a short symptom / how-found /\nroot-cause / change / regression-check format, shipped with one real worked\nexample (the #223 FileHandler leak) so the page never ships empty.\n\nReaches ddd-service-native-db, ddd-service-orm-db, mvc-service-native-db and\nmvc-service-orm-db via the shared scaffold_copy_docs_extras step in\nbin/lib/scaffold_python_templates.sh (not one of the per-tier scaffold\nscripts), and is registered append-only in each tier''s mkdocs.yml nav.\nlib-minimal is excluded — its scaffold script owns its docs copy list\nindependently and is out of scope here (see PR body).\n\nCloses #240\n\n* fix(ci): assemble ts-common/src into the typecheck workspace\n\nThe react-spa-webpack typecheck job copied only the skeleton''s own\nsrc/ into the synthetic /tmp/ts-typecheck workspace, skipping the\nts-common/src/ overlay that copy_shared_ts_source() applies in the\nreal scaffold (blueprintx#436). context.tsx imports\n@/shared/utils/log-emitter (blueprintx#441), which lives only in\nts-common, so the job typechecked a workspace the real scaffold\nnever produces and failed with TS2307.\n\nMirror the real copy order: skeleton src first, then overlay\nts-common/src/ onto src/shared/.\n\n* fix(templates): list the incidents page in api and lib-minimal nav\n\nThe shared copy step ships docs/incidents.md to every Python tier, but\nonly the four service tiers listed it in mkdocs.yml nav, leaving the\npage orphaned in api-service-native-db and lib-minimal.\n\n* docs(templates): say five fields in the incident-record format\n\nThe Format section listed five fields and its example used five, but\nthe lead sentence said four.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>","tree":{"sha":"760fb217a48b5e13423cc10296f384f5e8f9dbef","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/trees/760fb217a48b5e13423cc10296f384f5e8f9dbef"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/commits/f85ddc3e929a49b5a694e4d438fd38522eb4835f","verification":{"payload":"tree 760fb217a48b5e13423cc10296f384f5e8f9dbef\nparent 35e9ce124ab1e1720305d7df9973fe2b0311545d\nauthor Guilherme Rodrigues <55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com> 1791328393 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791328393 -0300\n\nfeat(templates): ship a published incident-record page in docs/ (#316)\n\n* feat(templates): ship a published incident-record page in docs/\n\nAdds templates/python-common/docs/incidents.md — a short symptom / how-found /\nroot-cause / change / regression-check format, shipped with one real worked\nexa