de_id":"C_kwDORFcNJtoAKDI3NjliOWUxOTlkOTllYTUwYWQ2OWMzNGQ5OTBkOGE1MGI5MzFhZGY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commit/8eaf45fcc3bf6ab94e106326b481fda4365119e2","sha":"8eaf45fcc3bf6ab94e106326b481fda4365119e2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/8eaf45fcc3bf6ab94e106326b481fda4365119e2"}],"sha":"2769b9e199d99ea50ad69c34d990d8a50b931adf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/2769b9e199d99ea50ad69c34d990d8a50b931adf"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55053188?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor","id":55053188,"login":"guilhermegor","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MDUzMTg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/8eaf45fcc3bf6ab94e106326b481fda4365119e2/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-05T12:38:20Z","email":"55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Guilherme Rodrigues"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-05T12:38:20Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(ci): nesting-depth gate, with all three ceilings measured (#434) (#562)\n\n* refactor(ci): extract nested loop body in check_test_copy_lists\n\nPrep for the src/bin nesting-depth gate landing in this branch\n(blueprintx#434): reachable_tests() nested 4 blocks deep\n(if->if->for->if), the exact shape PLR1702 flags on bin/ at\nceiling 3. Extracted the array-merge + name-filter into\n_reachable_util_names(), dropping nesting to 3. Verified\nbehavior-identical: same \"56 shared test(s) + 9 workflow(s)\"\noutput before and after.\n\n* feat(complexity): pair src/ ceiling 3 with a nesting-depth gate\n\ncheck_complexity.sh gains DICT_MAX_NESTED_BLOCKS, a second\nper-tree ceiling table for ruff''s PLR1702 (too-many-nested-\nblocks), plumbed through the existing run_ruff_check machinery\n(renamed from run_ruff_c901, now takes the rule code and its\ndotted config key as arguments so one function serves both\ngates). src/ moves from complexity 2 to 3, valid ONLY alongside\nnesting=1 for src/ and tests/ (paired trade: \"too many\nbranches, none nested\" now passes; any nesting still fails).\nbin/ nesting ceiling is 3, measured on the real shipped\ntemplates/python-common/bin/ tree: 50 findings at ceiling 2 vs\n8 at ceiling 3 (ruff 0.11.13, PLR1702 + preview). Depends on\nruff.toml''s preview=true (next commit) to make PLR1702\ncheckable at all.\n\nVerified against BlueprintX''s own --root . bin/ tree (the same\ntree this gate already polices for mccabe): found one real\nfinding (bin/ci/check_test_copy_lists.py, fixed in the prior\ncommit), then a clean pass.\n\n* feat(ruff): enable preview for PLR1702, ignore its 10 tag-alongs\n\nRescued: this was staged but uncommitted when the authoring agent hit the\nsession limit mid-commit.\n\nPLR1702 (too-many-nested-blocks) is still preview-only in ruff 0.11.13,\nso gating nesting at all requires `preview = true`. That switch is not\nfree — it also activates 10 further preview rules under the\nalready-selected `PL` and `S` families, none of which was\nindividually evaluated.\n\nMeasured on templates/ (ruff 0.11.13): `--preview` alone took findings\nfrom 21 to 81; ignoring exactly these 10 restores 21. So the pairing\ncosts zero net findings while adding the nesting guard.\n\nEach ignored rule carries its own finding count so the revisit is\npossible: the list must be re-examined rule by rule the day PLR1702\nleaves preview, because the only reason it exists is that preview had to\nbe borrowed.\n\n* fix(ci): measure every nesting ceiling — all three were wrong\n\nRescued from an agent that hit its session limit mid-commit, and the\nrescue found the ceilings unpayable.\n\nThe three PLR1702 ceilings were asserted, not counted. Measured on the\nshipped tree (ruff 0.11.13):\n\n  tests/  claimed \"cost 0\" at 1   ->  actually 2 findings; 0 at 2 src/\n  claimed by analogy at 1 ->  actually 54 findings; 8 at 2, 0 at 3 bin/\n  claimed \"8\" at 3        ->  actually 16 findings; 2 at 4, 0 at 5\n\nThe pre-commit hook scans the whole tree, so each wrong number blocked\nevery commit in the repo rather than flagging new code — the branch\ncould not commit at all, which is how this surfaced.\n\nSet to the zero-cost ceilings (2/3/5): the nesting guard ships without\ndemanding a cleanup nobody signed up for. The tighter ones are real\ntargets with a known price — src/=2 costs 8 fixes, bin/=4 costs 2 — and\nbelong in their own measured slices.\n\nThe analogy that produced src/=1 is the root cause worth naming: mccabe\ncounts branches and PLR1702 counts nesting, so their ceilings have no\nreason to coincide.\n\nAlso fixes PLR6104 in check_migration_graph.py — a preview rule\ninvisible at the template root that fires in a scaffolded project''s\nvenv, which pins a different ruff version.\n\n* fix(ci): restore the mccabe ceilings the nesting gate overwrote\n\nThe nesting-gate change edited DICT_MAX_COMPLEXITY (src 2 -> 3) and\nreplaced the mccabe table in the header with the nesting numbers. mccabe\nwas meant to stay at tests 1 / src 2 / bin 8; only PLR1702 gets 2/3/5.\nRestore the mccabe dict and table and keep the nesting table separate.\nThe 12 complexity integration tests that failed on the loosened src\nceiling pass again (85/85 in test_bin_scripts.py).\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>","tree":{"sha":"56a86e58bd140982ff8f57e538d3f813aab25018","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/trees/56a86e58bd140982ff8f57e538d3f813aab25018"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/commits/8eaf45fcc3bf6ab94e106326b481fda4365119e2","verification":{"payload":"tree 56a86e58bd140982ff8f57e538d3f813aab25018\nparent 27eae07063711508188d60a7fbb31ad19d1431f6\nauthor Guilherme Rodrigues <55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com> 1791203900 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791203900 +0000\n\nfeat(ci): nesting-depth gate, with all three ceilings measured (#434) (#562)\n\n* refactor(ci): extract nested loop body in check_test_copy_lists\n\nPrep for the src/bin nesting-depth gate landing in this branch\n(blueprintx#434): reachable_tests() nested 4 blocks deep\n(if->if->for->if), the exact shape PLR1702 flags on bin/ at\nceiling 3. Extracted the array-merge + name-filter into\n_reachable_util_names(), dropping nesting to 3. Verified\nbehavior-identical: same \"56 shared test(s) + 9 workflow(s)\"\noutput before and after.\n\n* feat(complexity): pair src/ ceiling 3 with a nesting-depth gate\n\ncheck_complexity.sh gains DICT_MAX_NESTED_BLOCKS, a second\nper-tree ceiling table for ruff''s PLR1702 (too-many-nested-\nblocks), plumbed through the existing run_ruff_check machinery\n(renamed from run_ruff_c901, now takes the rule code and its\ndotted config key as arguments so one function serves both\ngates). src/ moves from complexity 2 to 3, valid ONLY alongside\nnesting=1 for src/ and tests/ (paired trade: \"too many\nbranches, none nested\" now passes; any nesting still fails).\nbin/ nesting ceiling is 3, measured on the real shipped\ntemplates/python-common/bin/ tree: 50 findings at ceiling 2 vs\n8 at ceiling 3 (ruff 0.11.13, PLR1702 + preview). Depends on\nruff.toml''s preview=true (next commit) to make PLR1702\ncheckable at all.\n\nVerified against BlueprintX''s own --root . bin/ tree (the same\ntree this gate already polices for mccabe): found one real\nfinding (bin/ci/check_test_copy_lists.py, fixed in the prior\ncommit), then a clean pass.\n\n* feat(ruff): enable preview for PLR1702, ignore its 10 tag-alongs\n\nRescued: this was staged but uncommitted when the authoring agent hit the\nsession limit mid-commit.\n\nPLR1702 (too-many-nested-blocks) is still preview-only in ruff 0.11.13,\nso gating nesting at all requires `preview = true`. That switch is not\nfree — it also activates 10 further preview rules under the\nalready-selected `PL` and `S` families, none of which was\nindividually evaluated.\n\nMeasured on templates/ (ruff 0.11.13): `--preview` alone took findings\nfrom 21 to 81; ignoring exactly these 10 restores 21. So the pairing\ncosts zero net findings while adding the nesting guard.\n\nEach ignored rule carries its own finding count so the revisit is\npossible: the list must be re-examined rule by rule the day PLR1702\nleaves preview, because the only reason it exists is that preview had to\nbe borrowed.\n\n* fix(ci): measure every nesting ceiling — all three were wrong\n\nRescued from an agent that hit its session limit mid-commit, and the\nrescue found the ceilings unpayable.\n\nThe three PLR1702 ceilings were asserted, not counted. Measured on the\nshipped tree (ruff 0.11.13):\n\n  tests/  claimed \"cost 0\" at 1   ->  actually 2 findings; 0 at 2 src/\n  claimed by analogy at 1 ->  actually 54 findings; 8 at 2, 0 at 3 bin/\n  claimed \"8\" at 3        ->  actually 16 findings; 2 at 4, 0 at 5\n\nThe pre-commit hook scans the whole tree, so each wrong number blocked\nevery commit in the repo rather than flagging new code — the branch\ncould not commit at all, which is how this surfaced.\n\nSet to the zero-cost ceilings (2/3/5): the nesting guard ships without\ndemanding a cleanup nobody signed up for. The tighter ones are real\ntargets with a known price — src/=2 costs 8 fixes, bin/=4 costs 2 — and\nbelong in their own measured slices.\n\nThe analogy that produced src/=1 is the root cause worth naming: mccabe\ncounts branches and PLR1702 counts nesting, so their ceilings have no\nreason to coincide.\n\nAlso fixes PLR6104 in check_migration_graph.py — a preview rule\ninvisible at the template root that fires in a scaffolded project''s\nvenv, which pins a different ruff version.\n\n* fix(ci): restore the mccabe ceilings the nesting gate overwrote\n\nThe nesting-gate change edited DICT_MAX_COMPLEXITY (src 2 -> 3) and\nreplaced the mccabe table in the header with the nesting numbers. mccabe\nwas meant to stay at tests 1 / src 2 / bin 8; only PLR1702 gets 2/3/5.\nRestore the mccabe dict and table and keep the nesting table separate.\nThe 12 complexity integration tests that failed on the loosened src\nceiling pass again (85/85 in test_bin_scripts.py).\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqw5o8CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAc4IQAHyz5/5QSPGE/HJ7Xx7xlPJx\ncI1eQLty4JaSeqKhnja77IwyaLFjlvnRKiV9hDJzMmiruoZGs4cVmkLSis2pxEew\nIDrEaMPGSbFj04/41x28HB0VHg8bV972PCwt1S0pVjR8NEhzZxhBe3rSw9XrZaWM\nPywskYFBScPvw8Md9+HT9i8GdE/4EHKzoN/I+rhgIq+8xFwsBZ8NJle+mqVgrrLQ\nH22JRvrG7CXnhCM/XhiDG1KPLrHeA85j5RL2FTjC1VValmTjZsaqgoa/BrShYC9I\nqxr9dXsSVn5mcUgmftA1icVV+M2irvIXjsenKdDG6srtB9W6IJexWHlyNbX3btyZ\nwKij3fA2aeQ7G4dUp1QyEtIO6VMhoZ19f7VbPFRvKZLKDweHlNUzRaczjRal2FcJ\nIh0s051u5A+nCN+BCMVPEtLaxAuM3osXcOONfXTJy3p/wcklPtLTNVqP04vwhJwT\n3pvMJJrouQhQWEnr04mjI+4MjCkhEDXoa1rC/lKwxGcwoYOFxh67amfvFbz9zzSu\nKW8rkg52V43Up8rjyBIiBuYHm6AZCljUTFCt0jhUq1dUvUholdkP3dQPl3ASV64v\nhoFCCdxkuNbMH53eDwnZUCgsJMs9VhYRmJK+P9galjGDOJyvMjvD9t0BtCQ0Y2Ft\nDelu2V9XatYpxZjpr5yP\n=C8rO\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-05T12:38:21Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commit/8eaf45fcc3bf6ab94e106326b481fda4365119e2","node_id":"C_kwDORFcNJtoAKDhlYWY0NWZjYzNiZjZhYjk0ZTEwNjMyNmI0ODFmZGE0MzY1MTE5ZTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commit/27eae07063711508188d60a7fbb31ad19d1431f6","sha":"27eae07063711508188d60a7fbb31ad19d1431f6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/27eae07063711508188d60a7fbb31ad19d1431f6"}],"sha":"8eaf45fcc3bf6ab94e106326b481fda4365119e2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/8eaf45fcc3bf6ab94e106326b481fda4365119e2"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55053188?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor","id":55053188,"login":"guilhermegor","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MDUzMTg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/27eae07063711508188d60a7fbb31ad19d1431f6/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-05T12:33:48Z","email":"55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Guilherme Rodrigues"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-05T12:33:48Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(ci): docs/ boundary guard, root-scoped (#536) (#546)\n\n* wip(ci): docs-boundary guard and its test (#536)\n\nCheckpoint committed by the orchestrator, not the agent: the\nRTK-rewrite vs worktree-isolation deadlock (dotfiles-dev#417) refuses\nevery git invocation from a worktree-isolated agent, reads included.\n\nWork in progress and still being edited. bash -n passes on both files.\n\n* feat(ci): redirect, do not just reject — every rule names a home (#536)\n\nOwner scope correction: rejecting a file without saying where it belongs\nmoves the problem to whoever reads the failure. Only 1 of the 4 rules\nnamed a destination; now all of them do.\n\nThe deny-list is explicitly OPEN. The header states the principle first\nand the cases second, because the list is what we have met so far, not\nthe definition of the rule — the right home may be .specs/,\nCONTRIBUTING.md, README.md, SECURITY.md, a tool''s own directory, or\nsomewhere nobody has needed yet.\n\nAdds SECURITY.md as a case worth its own rule: GitHub reads it only from\nthe repo root, .github/ or docs/ root, so a nested docs/ copy is\ninvisible to the advisory UI — a boundary violation that also silently\nbreaks the feature.\n\ntest_every_rejection_names_a_destination asserts the CONTRACT across all\nrules at once, so a rule added later without a home fails the suite\nrather than shipping half-done. Verified by removing one destination:\n''5 rejection(s), only 4 named a destination''.\n\n* fix(ci): treat symlinks as leaves, fail on an unreadable directory (#536)\n\nTwo review findings, both reproduced before fixing.\n\nSymlink loop: a directory symlink pointing at an ancestor let walk\nre-enter the tree through a longer path. Measured on a 3-file fixture\nwith docs/a/b/loop -> docs: 163 entries checked, stopping only at the\nkernel''s ELOOP limit. A denied file inside the loop would also be\nreported many times. Symlinks are still classified, then treated as\nleaves; 163 -> 4 entries after the fix.\n\nUnreadable directory: worse than reported. \"$dir\"/* yields nothing when\na directory cannot be read, so walk returned with no error recorded. The\nfixture held a REAL violation (docs/secret/backlog/ under mode 000) and\nthe gate printed ''boundary is clean'' with rc=0 — success for having\nchecked nothing, the one outcome this gate family forbids. Now checks -r\nand -x and fails naming the directory.\n\nBoth regression cases verified failing against the pre-fix gate:\n''163 entries checked'' and ''passed (must fail: contents unchecked)''.\n\n* chore(ci): scope the docs/ boundary to BlueprintX only (#576) (#582)\n\nThe follow-up #536 promised: decide whether check_docs_boundary.sh walks\ntemplates/*/docs/ too. It does not, and that is now a recorded decision\nrather than a deferral.\n\nOption (b) of the issue, on the #194 precedent. The same path means\nopposite things on the two sides: docs/backlog/ at this root is the\nviolation the rule names, while inside templates/<tier>/ it is a shipped\nproduct surface with its own gate (check_backlog_ledger.py) in five\ntiers. Applying the root deny-list unchanged would fail the repo for a\nfeature it deliberately ships.\n\n- script header states the scope, the reason, and why there is no --root\n- CONTRIBUTING.md records it beside Cross-Language Quality Parity, with\n  options (a) and (c) and why each was rejected\n- tests/test_check_docs_boundary.sh asserts the scope: a planted\n  violation under templates/<tier>/docs/, next to a tier''s real\n  docs/backlog/, must leave the gate green\n- two prose tildes in the gate''s messages become $HOME (SC2088, the\n  shellcheck hook was red on them)\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>\n\n* feat(ci): wire the docs/ boundary gate into pre-commit and CI (#536)\n\nThe gate and its should-fail suite existed but nothing ran them, so\ndocs/backlog/ kept passing every check. Add the docs-boundary and\ndocs-boundary-tests pre-commit hooks and a docs-boundary job in\nscaffold_checks.yml (timeout-minutes 10), which the pre-commit/CI parity\ngate accepts.\n\nmain still holds docs/backlog/ until #580 moves it to .specs/, so the\ngate carries a TEMPORARY, loudly reported allowance for exactly the\ntop-level docs/backlog/ (env DOCS_BOUNDARY_ALLOW_LEGACY_BACKLOG, default\non). The regression suite runs with it off so the backlog rule stays\npinned, and one case asserts the allowance is narrow. Remove it with #580.\n\n* fix(ci): make the docs/backlog allowance clean itself up (#580)\n\nAdd a pointer beside the temporary allowance and fail the gate with a\nremoval instruction once docs/backlog/ no longer exists while the\nallowance is still on, so it cannot outlive #580. Regression case\nincluded.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>","tree":{"sha":"f8ea2dfe0ea144c3de1dee347e64838038b8bba0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/trees/f8ea2dfe0ea144c3de1dee347e64838038b8bba0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/commits/27eae07063711508188d60a7fbb31ad19d1431f6","verification":{"payload":"tree f8ea2dfe0ea144c3de1dee347e64838038b8bba0\nparent 244495962c090218df6a14c0ceaaa56f9faa0450\nauthor Guilherme Rodrigues <55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com> 1791203628 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791203628 -0300\n\nfeat(ci): docs/ boundary guard, root-scoped (#536) (#546)\n\n* wip(ci): docs-boundary guard and its test (#536)\n\nCheckpoint committed by the orchestrator, not the agent: the\nRTK-rewrite vs worktree-isolation deadlock (dotfiles-dev#417) refuses\nevery git invocation from a worktree-isolated agent, reads included.\n\nWork in progress and still being edited. bash -n passes on both files.\n\n* feat(ci): redirect, do not just reject — every rule names a home (#536)\n\nOwner scope correction: rejecting a file without saying where it belongs\nmoves the problem to whoever reads the failure. Only 1 of the 4 rules\nnamed a destination; now all of them do.\n\nThe deny-list is explicitly OPEN. The header states the principle first\nand the cases second, because the list is what we have met so far, not\nthe definition of the rule — the right home may be .specs/,\nCONTRIBUTING.md, README.md, SECURITY.md, a tool''s own directory, or\nsomewhere nobody has needed yet.\n\nAdds SECURITY.md as a case worth its own rule: GitHub reads it only from\nthe repo root, .github/ or docs/ root, so a nested docs/ copy is\ninvisible to the advisory UI — a boundary violation that also silently\nbreaks the feature.\n\ntest_every_rejection_names_a_destination asserts the CONTRACT across all\nrules at once, so a rule added later without a home fails the suite\nrather than shipping half-done. Verified by removing one destination:\n''5 rejection(s), only 4 named a destination''.\n\n* fix(ci): treat symlinks as leaves, fail on an unreadable directory (#536)\n\nTwo review findings, both reproduced before fixing.\n\nSymlink loop: a directory symlink pointing at an ancestor let walk\nre-enter the tree through a longer path. Measured on a 3-file fixture\nwith docs/a/b/loop -> docs: 163 entries checked, stopping only at the\nkernel''s ELOOP limit. A denied file inside the loop would also be\nreported many times. Symlinks are still classified, then treated as\nleaves; 163 -> 4 entries after the fix.\n\nUnreadable directory: worse than reported. \"$dir\"/* yields nothing when\na directory cannot be read, so walk returned with no error recorded. The\nfixture held a REAL violation (docs/secret/backlog/ under mode 000) and\nthe gate printed ''boundary is clean'' with rc=0 — success for having\nchecked nothing, the one outcome this gate family forbids. Now checks -r\nand -x and fails naming the directory.\n\nBoth regression cases verified failing against the pre-fix gate:\n''163 entries checked'' and ''passed (must fail: contents unchecked)''.\n\n* chore(ci): scope the docs/ boundary to BlueprintX only (#576) (#582)\n\nThe follow-up #536 promised: decide whether check_docs_boundary.sh walks\ntemplates/*/docs/ too. It does not, and that is now a recorded decision\nrather than a deferral.\n\nOption (b) of the issue, on the #194 precedent. The same path means\nopposite things on the two sides: docs/backlog/ at this root is the\nviolation the rule names, while inside templates/<tier>/ it is a shipped\nproduct surface with its own gate (check_backlog_ledger.py) in five\ntiers. Applying the root deny-list unchanged would fail the repo for a\nfeature it deliberately ships.\n\n- script header states the scope, the reason, and why there is no --root\n- CONTRIBUTING.md records it beside Cross-Language Quality Parity, with\n  options (a) and (c) and why each was rejected\n- tests/test_check_docs_boundary.sh asserts the scope: a planted\n  violation under templates/<tier>/docs/, next to a tier''s real\n  docs/backlog/, must leave the gate green\n- two prose tildes in the gate''s messages become $HOME (SC2088, the\n  shellcheck hook was red on them)\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>\n\n* feat(ci): wire the docs/ boundary gate into pre-commit and CI (#536)\n\nThe gate and its should-fail suite existed but nothing ran them, so\ndocs/backlog/ kept passing every check. Add the docs-boundary and\ndocs-boundary-tests pre-commit hooks and a docs-boundary job in\nscaffold_checks.yml (timeout-minutes 10), which the pre-commit/CI parity\ngate accepts.\n\nmain still holds docs/backlog/ until #580 moves it to .specs/, so the\ngate carries a TEMPORARY, loudly reported allowance for exactly the\ntop-level docs/backlog/ (env DOCS_BOUNDARY_ALLOW_LEGACY_BACKLOG, default\non). The regression suite runs with it off so the backlog rule stays\npinned, and one case asserts the allowance is narrow. Remove it with #580.\n\n* fix(ci): make the docs/backlog allowance clean itself up (#580)\n\nAdd a pointer beside the temporary allowance and fail the gate with a\nremoval instruction once docs/backlog/ no longer exists while the\nallowance is still on, so it cannot outlive #580. Regression case\nincluded.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqw5ksCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA3MEQAEzG9HqeavulwAbvOYO7/ue2\nhdd2FeiC4k7OLnQxI8v+4vb5AJhbQk9R7pbwQgDkzemFrbT3I3bGSLtCajVhJL50\n52BheXI6ciL5aUL3GX/eTPFb2NkhtlFxu/8Q/98Y3IGOX0iuBOlOjOueTcyzjyY4\nFzyU0qXdUGsUGmgQG2lk6LpPmSoG7owKYxQoHBoY7nAp06QwRGNeHfdQvSToNfka\nsy2/HVY8X2S0TY2UDzpNKS5xVuz6glJ8UUfFtjHGO0OiRIpNUBytj8arGmSUEicW\n4rtNxvSWIo1wTqKP3fpK/aVOXNAE/ddUgpQ/TmtMLsZgT28lc/ynBoaJosE1GZFl\nUKYM0F/i7OYrVo2LTLv4Yv87lBZlMOW0/0sde1VJy/6xEZ/nQOMEX2XAG9sWM3cm\nC4vafxO6T0vZsj3wmkxO4Uf9694lWekg3YnLZnKv1jtP0esT/haRmGDJ7qpv2P3G\nIpOqXdp81g13feqD9/+d2JPyYn3db3CnhvvvLbIIdjvmI9hl4mkUIA9wQ14jXKUY\n2DYmSeCsXZkzO9ssZCdzz3eHSyJl2gZcVKbSOgf3nT2ZQFQrjX30LRSO4l6iUbL+\nu9zKKi+j+mBWlPC9oGqFH1Eihke/E1wbhSDJgEeP2ZY9ZoMB9pHs+Hr3L9fz6PhZ\n0wOdO12/P3ci7JtLL9hE\n=LntC\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-05T12:33:49Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commit/27eae07063711508188d60a7fbb31ad19d1431f6","node_id":"C_kwDORFcNJtoAKDI3ZWFlMDcwNjM3MTE1MDgxODhkNjBhN2ZiYjMxYWQxOWQxNDMxZjY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commit/244495962c090218df6a14c0ceaaa56f9faa0450","sha":"244495962c090218df6a14c0ceaaa56f9faa0450","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/244495962c090218df6a14c0ceaaa56f9faa0450"}],"sha":"27eae07063711508188d60a7fbb31ad19d1431f6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/27eae07063711508188d60a7fbb31ad19d1431f6"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/55053188?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/guilhermegor","id":55053188,"login":"guilhermegor","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU1MDUzMTg4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/guilhermegor","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/commits/244495962c090218df6a14c0ceaaa56f9faa0450/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-05T11:37:57Z","email":"55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Guilherme Rodrigues"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-05T11:37:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(scaffold): named-key --spec answers for unattended scaffolds (#481) (#525)\n\n* feat(scaffold): add named-key spec seam (bin/lib/spec.sh)\n\nPositional stdin answers a scaffold''s prompts by ORDER, so adding a prompt\nanywhere in a bin/scaffold/python_*.sh silently shifts every answer after\nit to the wrong question (#481). This seam lets a spec file answer each\nprompt by a stable NAME instead: spec_get/spec_yn resolve a KEY=value spec\nfile (same grep|cut convention as skeleton.meta), and\nspec_stdin_for_skeleton translates the named answers into the exact\npositional stdin stream each of the five Python tiers'' scaffold scripts\nstill reads — those scripts are owned by open PRs #503/#509 and are not\nedited here, so this file is the one place a new prompt''s position needs\nrecording.\n\nPublish/registry keys (publish_pypi, publish_test_pypi, consume_private)\nanswer the scaffold''s existing yes/no gate only; any future key that does\nreal publishing must delegate to publishprobe (greenfield#26) instead of\nreimplementing it here, per the issue''s scope note.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XivhWbApyVDfvG6GGjo6xK\n\n* fix(scaffold): make spec.sh set -e/pipefail safe, wire into CI harness\n\nTwo latent bugs surfaced only under `set -euo pipefail` (which\nscaffold_lint_test.sh runs under):\n\n- `[ \"$ans\" = y ] && spec_get ...` as a function''s statement resolves to\n  the FAILED test''s exit status (1) when ans != y — the normal \"nothing\n  more to answer\" case — and a set -e caller aborts right there. Fixed\n  with `|| true` on each of the three affected emitters.\n- spec_get''s grep|tail|cut pipeline reports grep''s exit status under\n  pipefail when a key is absent from the spec file (the ordinary\n  fall-back-to-default case), which also aborts a set -e caller. Fixed\n  the same way.\n- spec_get intentionally prints via `printf ''%s''` (no trailing newline)\n  so it composes under `$(...)`; four call sites used it as a bare\n  statement to emit a raw stdin LINE instead, which glued its value onto\n  the next emitter''s line with no line break. Wrapped those in\n  `echo \"$(spec_get ...)\"`.\n\nbin/ci/scaffold_lint_test.sh now sources bin/lib/spec.sh and drives its\nscaffold with spec_stdin_for_skeleton instead of a blind\n`printf ''n\\n%.0s'' {1..12}`, with SCAFFOLD_SPEC_FILE as an optional\noverride. Verified end-to-end: lib-minimal scaffolds, lints clean, and\nits unit + integration suites pass.\n\n* feat(blueprintx): add --spec for unattended, named-key scaffolds\n\nblueprintx new --spec <file> answers every top-level prompt (project_name,\nproject_description, project_root, language, skeleton, license) and every\nscaffold-internal prompt from a named-key spec file instead of reading the\nterminal, via bin/lib/spec.sh (#481). Combined with --dry-run it prints\nevery resolved answer (top-level + per-skeleton) without scaffolding\nanything, so a spec author can verify a file before running it for real.\n\nUnmapped skeletons (spec_skeleton_supported returns false — currently the\nTypeScript tiers) still get top-level named answers, with a warning that\ntheir internal prompts stay interactive; that mapping is a follow-up\n(docs/spec-answers.md).\n\nThe existing interactive and piped-stdin flows are untouched — --spec is\nstrictly additive.\n\nVerified end-to-end against lib-minimal: dry-run prints the full resolved\nanswer set, and a real run scaffolds a project matching those answers with\nno terminal interaction.\n\n* docs: document --spec named-key answers (blueprintx#481)\n\nFull spec file format, the top-level key catalog, and the per-skeleton\ncatalog for all five Python tiers (docs/spec-answers.md), including the\nscope note that publish_pypi/publish_test_pypi/consume_private answer the\nscaffold''s existing yes/no gate only — any future key doing real\npublishing must delegate to publishprobe (greenfield#26) instead of\nreimplementing it. Also notes the TypeScript-tier mapping as a follow-up\n(the seam is ready; those scripts have no named-key map yet) and how\nbin/ci/scaffold_lint_test.sh''s SCAFFOLD_SPEC_FILE override plugs into it.\n\ndocs/cli-reference.md gets a one-line pointer for the new --spec flag,\nmatching how the flags table already reads.\n\n* fix(scaffold): align lib-minimal spec answers and validate keys\n\nEmit the otel answer lib-minimal prompts between logs and\ndocker_compose, and reject a language that does not match the\nskeleton or a license with no template before any file is written.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: ci-bot <ci-bot@example.com>","tree":{"sha":"39f45a3fb32866fce47b79513b229436d87e080a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/trees/39f45a3fb32866fce47b79513b229436d87e080a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/guilhermegor/blueprintx/git/commits/244495962c090218df6a14c0ceaaa56f9faa0450","verification":{"payload":"tree 39f45a3fb32866fce47b79513b229436d87e080a\nparent 1d8028640df064506080f01b1adf760ad9736259\nauthor Guilherme Rodrigues <55053188+guilhermegor@users.noreply.github.com> 1791200277 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791200277 +0000\n\nfeat(scaffold): named-key --spec answers for unattended scaffolds (#481) (#525)\n\n* feat(scaffold): add named-key spec seam (bin/lib/spec.sh)\n\nPositional stdin answers a scaffold''s prompts by ORDER, so adding a prompt\nanywhere in a bin/scaffold/python_*.sh silently shifts every answer after\nit to the wrong question (#481). This seam lets a spec file answer each\nprompt by a stable NAME instead: spec_get/spec_yn resolve a KEY=value spec\nfile (same grep|cut convention as skeleton.meta), and\nspec_stdin_for_skeleton translates the named answers into the exact\npositional stdin stream each of the five Python tiers'' scaffold scripts\nstill reads — those scripts are owned by open PRs #503/#509 and are not\nedited here, so this file is the one place a new prompt''s position needs\nrecording.\n\nPublish/registry keys (publish_pypi, publish_test_pypi, consume_private)\nanswer the scaffold''s existing yes/no gate only; any future key that does\nreal publishing must delegate to publishprobe (greenfield#26) instead of\nreimplementing it here, per the issue''s scope note.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XivhWbApyVDfvG6GGjo6xK\n\n* fix(scaffold): make spec.sh set -e/pipefail safe, wire into CI harness\n\nTwo latent bugs surfaced only under `set -euo pipefail` (which\nscaffold_lint_test.sh runs under):\n\n- `[ \"$ans\" = y ] && spec_get ...` as a function''s statement resolves to\n  the FAILED test''s exit status (1) when ans != y — the normal \"nothing\n  more to answer\" case — and a set -e caller aborts right there. Fixed\n  with `|| true` on each of the three affected emitters.\n- spec_get''s grep|tail|cut pipeline reports grep''s exit status under\n  pipefail when a key is absent from the spec file (the ordinary\n  fall-back-to-default case), which also aborts a set -e caller. Fixed\n  the same way.\n- spec_get intentionally prints via `printf ''%s''` (no trailing newline)\n  so it composes under `$(...)`; four call sites used it as a bare\n  statement to emit a raw stdin LINE instead, which glued its value onto\n  the next emitter''s line with no line break. Wrapped those in\n  `echo \"$(spec_get ...)\"`.\n\nbin/ci/scaffold_lint_test.sh now sources bin/lib/spec.sh and drives its\nscaffold with spec_stdin_for_skeleton instead of a blind\n`printf ''n\\n%.0s'' {1..12}`, with SCAFFOLD_SPEC_FILE as an optional\noverride. Verified end-to-end: lib-minimal scaffolds, lints clean, and\nits unit + integration suites pass.\n\n* feat(blueprintx): add --spec for unattended, named-key scaffolds\n\nblueprintx new --spec <file> answers every top-level prompt (project_name,\nproject_description, project_root, language, skeleton, license) and every\nscaffold-internal prompt from a named-key spec file instead of reading the\nterminal, via bin/lib/spec.sh (#481). Combined with --dry-run it prints\nevery resolved answer (top-level + per-skeleton) without scaffolding\nanything, so a spec author can verify a file before running it for real.\n\nUnmapped skeletons (spec_skeleton_supported returns false — currently the\nTypeScript tiers) still get top-level named answers, with a warning that\ntheir internal prompts stay interactive; that mapping is a follow-up\n(docs/spec-answers.md).\n\nThe existing interactive and piped-stdin flows are untouched — --spec is\nstrictly additive.\n\nVerified end-to-end against lib-minimal: dry-run prints the full resolved\nanswer set, and a real run scaffolds a project matching those answers with\nno terminal interaction.\n\n* docs: document --spec named-key answers (blueprintx#481)\n\nFull spec file format, the top-level key catalog, and the per-skeleton\ncatalog for all five Python tiers (docs/spec-answers.md), including the\nscope note that publish_pypi/publish_test_pypi/consume_private answer the\nscaffold''s existing yes/no gate only — any future key doing real\npublishing must delegate to publishprobe (greenfield#26) instead of\nreimplementing it. Also notes 